session-close's narrative check read the wrong line, and was green by coincidence
2026-08-09, closing Phase 20e.0

THE SYMPTOM
  make session-close on the devbox, after both patches were pushed:

    session-close: docs/discussion-log.md says 2026-08-08, the newest session
    session-close: is 2026-08-09 - the narrative is behind the cursor

  The narrative was NOT behind the cursor. docs/discussion-log.md line 9 read
  "**As of 2026-08-09 (20e.0 - the dashboard is composed, not scrolled).**",
  pushed in the same patch that produced the row it was being compared against.

THE CAUSE
  The check required the date to be followed immediately by ".**":

    grep -o '\*\*As of [0-9-]*\.\*\*' docs/discussion-log.md | head -1

  Every Current state block written since 2026-08-08 carries the phase in
  parentheses between the date and the closing "**", so none of them match. The
  expression fell through the whole file to line 514 - an entry from an earlier
  phase, in the older format - and read ITS date.

  Which lines each expression lands on, measured against the file exactly as it
  was pushed (git show origin/main:docs/discussion-log.md):

    old expression -> line 514: **As of 2026-08-08.**      reads 2026-08-08
    new expression -> line   9: **As of 2026-08-09         reads 2026-08-09

  Same file, same command, one clause changed. The two answers differ, which is
  the whole demonstration: until they differ, a gate reading the wrong line is
  indistinguishable from one reading the right line.

WHY IT HAD NEVER SPOKEN
  Line 514 says 2026-08-08. The newest session on 2026-08-08 was also dated
  2026-08-08. So on every close that day the check compared a stale line against
  a matching date and passed - not because the narrative was current, but
  because two unrelated things happened to carry the same number. Four sessions
  closed under it that day.

  It went red on the first day the coincidence broke. It would have gone red on
  2026-08-09 whatever the narrative said, and it would have stayed green on
  2026-08-08 whatever the narrative said. Neither verdict was about the file it
  claimed to be reading.

THE FIX
  Match the first "**As of <date>" whatever follows the date. The document
  convention is left alone: reformatting a dozen Current state blocks to suit a
  reader is fixing the wrong half, and this session started by removing a
  document that had been written to suit an assumption.

    stated="$(grep -o '\*\*As of [0-9]\{4\}-[0-9]\{2\}-[0-9]\{2\}' \
              docs/discussion-log.md | grep -o '[0-9-]\{10\}' | head -1)"

  A first attempt also rewrote the 2026-08-09 entry into the strict form, and
  then both expressions read 2026-08-09 and the comparison proved nothing. That
  reading is worthless in the same way the four green closes were: the
  instrument and the subject had both been changed. Reverted, and measured
  against the pushed file instead.

NOT COVERED
  The reader now takes the FIRST such line in the file and assumes newest-first
  ordering. Nothing checks that ordering. INDEX has a chronological check for
  exactly this reason and discussion-log does not; recorded, not fixed here.
